# API Design

## REST vs GraphQL: decide by consumer shape

- **REST by default** — single web app, simple CRUD-shaped resources,
  straightforward client needs.
- **GraphQL** when there are multiple/diverse consumers (e.g. mobile +
  web hitting the same backend with different data needs) where flexible
  querying avoids either over-fetching or a proliferation of
  purpose-built REST endpoints.
- Don't default to GraphQL for a single-consumer CRUD app just because
  it's more capable — the extra schema/resolver machinery isn't earning
  its cost there.

## REST resource design

```
# Resources are nouns, plural, kebab-case
GET    /api/users
GET    /api/users/:id
POST   /api/users
PATCH  /api/users/:id
DELETE /api/users/:id

# Sub-resources for ownership
GET    /api/users/:id/orders

# Verbs only for actions that don't map to CRUD
POST   /api/orders/:id/cancel
```

No verb in the URL for CRUD operations. No versioned prefix (see
Versioning below) until it's actually needed.

## Status codes — always semantic, never 200-for-everything

```
200 OK / 201 Created (with Location header) / 204 No Content
400 Bad Request        — malformed request
401 Unauthorized       — missing/invalid auth
403 Forbidden          — authenticated, not authorized
404 Not Found
409 Conflict           — duplicate/state conflict
422 Unprocessable Entity — semantically invalid data
429 Too Many Requests
500 Internal Server Error — never expose internals in the message
```

Never return `200` with a `success: false` body — the HTTP status code
carries that meaning; don't duplicate it in the payload.

## Response format: envelope with data wrapper

Default response shape, always:

```json
// Success
{ "data": { ... }, "meta": { ... }, "links": { ... } }

// Collection
{ "data": [...], "meta": { "total": 142, "page": 1, "per_page": 20 } }

// Error
{ "error": { "code": "validation_error", "message": "...", "details": [...] } }
```

Apply this consistently even for internal-only APIs — it costs nothing
extra to set up and means pagination/metadata can be added later without
a breaking response-shape change.

## Pagination

- **Offset-based** (`?page=2&per_page=20`) for admin dashboards and
  smaller datasets — simple, supports jump-to-page.
- **Cursor-based** (`?cursor=...&limit=20`) for anything public-facing,
  infinite scroll, or large/growing datasets — consistent performance,
  stable under concurrent inserts.
- Default to cursor for public APIs, offset for internal admin-style
  views — don't use offset pagination on a dataset expected to grow large
  or be publicly exposed.

## Error responses

Field-level detail on validation failures, always machine-readable
(`code`) plus human-readable (`message`):

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "validation_error",
    "message": "Request validation failed",
    "details": [{ "field": "email", "message": "Must be a valid email address", "code": "invalid_format" }]
  }
}
```

## Versioning: skip until a breaking change actually ships

No `/v1/` prefix by default — don't pay that cost upfront. Introduce
versioning only when a real breaking change needs to go out while
existing consumers keep working. At that point, URL-based versioning
(`/api/v2/...`) is the default choice — most widely understood, simplest
to route.

## Limitations

- This is single-service API design. Contract design between services in
  a distributed system (schema evolution across service boundaries,
  backward-compat guarantees for internal consumers) is covered by
  `warisskill-system-design-distributed-patterns` instead.
